{
  "id": "contextual-interference-effect",
  "term": "contextual interference effect",
  "definition": "The contextual interference effect is a motor learning phenomenon about how the amount of variation or interference built into practice influences later performance. In the source discussed here, lower interference was linked to better improvement on a closed skill test, while moderate interference was linked to better transfer of the skill to match-play, suggesting the effect may be less straightforward for complex skills.",
